## Proof

**Yes**, such a monoid exists. We construct one using the Novikov–Adian solution to the Burnside problem.

### Key Tool: Novikov–Adian Theorem

**Theorem (Novikov–Adian, 1968; improved by Adian, 1979):** For every odd integer $p \geq 665$ and every integer $m \geq 2$, the **free Burnside group** $B(m, p)$ — the quotient of the free group on $m$ generators by the normal subgroup generated by all $p$-th powers — is **infinite**.

This group $B(m,p)$ has **exponent** $p$: every element $g$ satisfies $g^p = 1$.

### The Construction

Set $G = B(2, p)$ for some fixed odd $p \geq 665$ (e.g., $p = 665$). We view $G$ as a monoid (every group is a monoid). We claim $G$ satisfies all the required properties.

**(1) $G$ is infinite.**  
By the Novikov–Adian theorem, $B(2, p)$ is infinite. ✓

**(2) $G$ is generated by $n = 2 > 1$ generators (as a monoid).**  
$G$ is generated as a group by two elements $a, b$. Since $G$ has exponent $p$, the inverse of any generator $g$ is $g^{p-1}$, which is a **positive** power of $g$. Therefore, every element of $G$ — being a product of generators and their inverses — can be written as a product of generators using only positive exponents. Hence $G$ is generated as a **monoid** by $\{a, b\}$, giving $n = 2 > 1$. ✓

**(3) $G$ is not a free monoid.**  
In a free monoid on any set of generators, the only **unit** (invertible element) is the identity. Indeed, if $w \cdot w' = \varepsilon$ (the empty word), then $|w| + |w'| = 0$, forcing $w = w' = \varepsilon$.  

But $G$ is a non-trivial group (it is infinite), so **every** element is a unit. A monoid in which every element is a unit can be a free monoid only if it is the trivial monoid $\{1\}$. Since $G$ is infinite, $G$ is not a free monoid. ✓

**(4) $G$ contains no free submonoid on $\geq 1$ generators.**  

We handle two cases:

*Free submonoid on 1 generator.* A free monoid on 1 generator is isomorphic to $(\mathbb{N}_0, +)$, which is infinite. If $M \subseteq G$ were such a submonoid, generated by some $g \in G$, then all powers $g^k$ ($k \geq 0$) would be distinct, meaning $g$ has **infinite order**. But every element of $G$ satisfies $g^p = 1$, so every element has **finite** order (dividing $p$). Contradiction.

*Free submonoid on $\geq 2$ generators.* Suppose $M \subseteq G$ is a submonoid that is free on a set $S$ with $|S| \geq 2$. Pick any generator $s \in S$. Since $s \in G$, we have $s^p = 1$ (the identity of $G$, which is the identity of $M$). In the free monoid on $|S| \geq 2$ generators, the relation $s^p = \varepsilon$ (the empty word) implies $|s|^p = 0$, hence $|s| = 0$, so $s = \varepsilon$. But $s$ is a **free generator**, so $s \neq \varepsilon$. Contradiction.

Therefore $G$ contains no free submonoid on any number of generators $\geq 1$. ✓

### Conclusion

The group $G = B(2, p)$ (for any odd $p \geq 665$), viewed as a monoid, is:
- **infinite** (by Novikov–Adian),
- **finitely generated** by $n = 2 > 1$ generators (inverses are positive powers due to bounded exponent),
- **not a free monoid** (it is a non-trivial group, hence has non-trivial units),
- **contains no free submonoid** on $\geq 1$ generators (all elements have finite order, precluding both $\mathbb{N}_0$ and non-commutative free submonoids).

This establishes the **existence** of such a monoid. $\blacksquare$

